What is ArrayList?
ArrayList is a class in Java’s java.util package.

It implements the List interface.

It’s a resizable array — unlike normal arrays, its size can grow or shrink dynamically.

🔹 Syntax
java
ArrayList<Type> list = new ArrayList<>();
Type → the type of elements you want to store (e.g., Integer, String, Double).

new ArrayList<>() → creates a new empty list object in memory.

Example:

java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
This creates an empty list that can hold String values.

🔹 Why <> (Diamond Operator)?
Before Java 7, you had to repeat the type:

java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();
Since Java 7, you can just write:

java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
The compiler infers the type (String here) automatically.

🔹 Key Features
Dynamic resizing: No need to specify size upfront.

Indexed access: You can access elements by index like arrays.

Allows duplicates: Unlike sets, lists can store duplicate values.

Maintains order: Elements stay in the order you insert them.



Common Methods
Method	Description	Example
add(E e)	Adds element	list.add("Hello")
get(int index)	Retrieves element	list.get(0)
set(int index, E e)	Updates element	list.set(0, "Hi")
remove(int index)	Removes element	list.remove(0)
size()	Returns number of elements	list.size()
contains(Object o)	Checks if element exists	list.contains("Hi")
clear()	Removes all elements	list.clear()


🔹 Example Program

import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);
        numbers.add(30);

        System.out.println(numbers);       // [10, 20, 30]
        System.out.println(numbers.get(1)); // 20
        numbers.remove(0);
        System.out.println(numbers);       // [20, 30]
    }
}



Visualizing ArrayList
Think of it like a flexible container:

Start empty: []

Add items: [10] → [10, 20] → [10, 20, 30]

Remove items: [20, 30]

